  "domain": "robotics",
  "definition": "A humanoid robot is an autonomous or semi-autonomous mechanical system whose overall morphology, kinematic chain, and sensorimotor organisation deliberately mirrors the human body plan: a vertical torso supported on two bipedal legs, bilateral upper limbs terminating in multi-fingered end-effectors, a head-mounted sensory cluster, and a centralised onboard compute stack, collectively enabling the robot to perceive, navigate, and manipulate a world that has been architecturally and ergonomically designed for human anatomy. Unlike wheeled or tracked mobile bases, humanoid robots must continuously solve the under-determined stability problem of bipedal locomotion\u2014balancing an inverted-pendulum-like structure with a high centre of mass on a small support polygon\u2014whilst simultaneously executing dexterous manipulation, perceiving a 3-D environment, and interpreting natural human instructions. This conjunction of simultaneous hard real-time control (joint torque bandwidths of 200-1000 Hz for balance), mid-frequency motion planning (1-50 Hz trajectory generation), and high-latency cognitive inference (0.1-10 Hz neural policy inference) across a single embodied platform represents the defin",
